A||||4||Fast Richard||Urethane fascia repair (again)||||5||
Z||000000||Fast Richard||07-10-2001||06:25 PM||||I have a couple small tears to fix in the rear fascia. After searching the archives, I became a wee bit confused. Ten people seem to recommend ten different products. A previous repair that seems to be working well is some epoxy-looking stuff with a metal screen reinforcement. The crack was then apparently veed out and a flexible, sandable filler was applied. Anyone have an update on the name of a product that, say the body shops would use? I really can't afford to pay $$$ for plastic welder. Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Voytek||07-10-2001||06:55 PM||||I've repaired a couple in the last few years and they're still perfect.  I find that Duramix works the best.  There are 3 or 4 different kinds of Duramix.  First, ask the body supply store (where you'll buy it) for the flexible type that dries in about 1 minute.  I believe this is an epoxy and comes in a double syringe-looking like tube (clear and black stuff that mix together).  You need to mix it fast.  Make sure the surfaces are sanded rough but clean.  Then, I used fiberglass cloth - excellent stuff.  I don't know where you can buy it - it came in a different body repair kit (for hard plastic bumper repairs).  It is a white pearl, shiny cloth.  Apply this stuff to the tear and glue it on with Duramix (from the BACK SIDE of the facia).  They say it dries in about a minute but that's a slight exaggeration.  It actually takes closer to 5 minutes before you can actually let go and I would recommend waiting about a day for it to harden properly.  Then you should apply plastic, flexible body filler on the outside of the facia.  Again, this stuff came in two tooth-paste like tubes (don't remember the name).  Excellent stuff - not that expensive.  One tube is white stuff, one tube is black stuff.  When mixed correctly, it produces a gray compound (no kidding, eh?).  This takes about 10 minutes to start setting.  You should probably re-apply 2 or 3 times to get the right thickness (it's hard to get it right with only one application).  This stuff is really easy to sand and flexible.  NOTE: try not to have the Duramix come out through the outside of the facia.  It is a lot tougher to sand and paint.  It should strictly be used to join the two surfaces (with the cloth for strength).  That's about it.  If you do it right, the fix will last for a long, long time (mine is about 4 years old, no cracks or deformation).<BR>Hope this helps.<BR>Voytek<BR>P.S. Duramix is the best stuff I've been able to find to-date.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||07-10-2001||07:04 PM||||After a woman pulled out in front of me on a highway and I hit her doing about 55-60 MPH, it caused two rips about 8" long each in my front fascia.  I used 3M 5887 that I got from the auto body supply house.  It has two tubes attached side-by-side and there is some sort of special gun that is made to squeeze the stuff out.  It has a weird tip that mixes the two parts as they are pressed out.  I just pushed equal amounts out by pressing the end of the tubes and mixed it to a consistant color.  I used fiberglass mat on the back of the fascia and put a thick layer back there to help with holding the tears closed.  Once that was dried, I applied the same stuff to the outside where I had "v-eed" the edges of the cuts.  Filled it in, let it dry and sanded it down.  It has been in place for 5-6 months now with no problems at all.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||07-11-2001||12:19 PM||||I use the 3M in my shop, buy it by the case. Its very flexible, Ive fixed some totally junk bumpers with it. I do let the first application dry overnite though before I touch it. You can use a heat gun or hair dryer to kick it if you want. (starts the catylizing a little quicker) but I still let it dry overnite. I prefer using the seperate tubes instead of the 'mixer' The mixers tend to leave 'soft' spots that dont harden.<P>part # 3M 5895B/5895A  ones white, ones black  makes a dark gray color mixed.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||88CoupeV6||07-11-2001||03:11 PM||||I found this stuff at autozone. It is a 2 part epoxy. It worked great on my bumper. Have had it since march before I had it painted. still like new.<P><BR>Here is a pic.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fbr.jpg">  <BR><P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
